

🧠 DFDL Domain — Short Notes

🔹 1. What is DFDL Domain (IIB/ACE)

Used to parse/serialize non-XML data (text, binary, COBOL, CSV, etc.)

Converts input → message tree, and message tree → output data

Works inside IIB/ACE message flows



---

🔹 2. Data Types (Logical Model)

Based on XSD types:
xs:string, xs:int, xs:decimal, xs:boolean, xs:date, etc.

Physical format control via DFDL annotations:
dfdl:representation, dfdl:textDatePattern, dfdl:textBooleanTrueRep, etc.

Example:

<xs:element name="age" type="xs:int" dfdl:representation="text"/>



---

🔹 3. Importing from C (Auto DFDL Schema)

Toolkit can import .h (C header file) → generate DFDL schema

Converts common C types → DFDL automatically

C Type	DFDL/XSD Type	Notes

int / int32_t	xs:int	Binary or text
uint32_t	xs:unsignedInt	
char name[20]	xs:string	dfdl:lengthKind="explicit" length="20"
float / double	xs:float / xs:double	Check byteOrder
Byte arrays	xs:hexBinary	Use for raw data




---

🔹 4. Import Restrictions / Fixes

Issue	Fix

Anonymous structs	Give proper names
Macros / preprocessor	Remove or preprocess (gcc -E file.h)
Alignment / padding	Add dfdl:alignment manually
Endianness issues	Set dfdl:byteOrder="littleEndian" or bigEndian"



---

🔹 5. Example (C → DFDL)

C struct:

typedef struct {
  uint32_t id;
  char name[20];
  int16_t age;
} PERSON;

Generated DFDL:

<xs:element name="PERSON">
  <xs:complexType>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="id" type="xs:unsignedInt"
                  dfdl:representation="binary" dfdl:byteOrder="littleEndian"/>
      <xs:element name="name" type="xs:string"
                  dfdl:lengthKind="explicit" dfdl:length="20"/>
      <xs:element name="age" type="xs:short"
                  dfdl:representation="binary" dfdl:byteOrder="littleEndian"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>
</xs:element>


---

🔹 6. Testing Workflow

1. Preprocess header file


2. Import into IIB Toolkit (C → DFDL)


3. Add annotations manually


4. Validate & test with DFDL Test Perspective


5. Adjust byte order, lengthKind, and alignment if parsing fails




